Power: one MeanWell RS-15-5 (5 V), three separate runs from its terminals — CM4,
Si5351/Teensy, ZED-F9T. AR-40A on its own LRS-75-15 (15 V), −V deliberately not
tied to the 5 V supply. See §5.

**Superseded arrangement**, kept so it isn't reintroduced: the AR-40A output used to
be split by a passive soldered T, unpadded, feeding the Extron and the Si5351 CLKIN
directly. That put 2.75 Vpp into a 1.5 Vpp-max input and gave the two destinations no
isolation from each other. Replaced by the pad plus a DA output per destination.

---

## 2b. Lock interlock — relay removed, replaced in firmware `[built]`

Previously: the AR-40A's BIT pin (DB9 pin 3) drove a relay coil — coil between +V and
the BIT pin, flyback diode across it — and the relay switched 5 V to the CM4, Teensy
and Si5351 board, with an LED indicating the same state. Ran for years. **Removed;
all three now power up directly.**

Note the AR-40A's 10 MHz output is present from power-up; the specified 5 minutes is
time to *lock*, not time to output. So the interlock was never required for the CM4
to boot.

Two consequences of removing it:

- **Warm-up frequency step.** The Si5351 now locks PLLA to the free-running OCXO
  (~10⁻⁷ off) at power-up, the CM4 boots on that, and the frequency shifts as the
  physics package pulls in ~5 min later. chrony copes but re-converges its frequency
  estimate after every boot.
- **No clean failure on a dead rubidium — MEASURED 2026-09-09.** `[meas]` With 5 V
  applied, the 10 MHz BNC open and the CM4 unpowered, CLK4 produces a periodic
  **~11.6 MHz** output (counter reading; confirmed by eye at 20 ns/div), 1.72 Vpp /
  686 mV rms into the CM4's clamps. The PLL loop is open, the charge pump rails, and
  the VCO parks around 186 MHz — MS4's ÷16 then gives 11.6 MHz. The 600–900 MHz VCO
  figure is the specified range *with* a reference, not a physical bound.

  This is the worst of the three possible outcomes: not silence, and not obviously
  broken, but a stable-looking clock at about a fifth of nominal. The CM4's internal
  PLLs are configured around 54 MHz and will likely not lock at all, so the symptom is
  a board with power that does nothing. Hard to diagnose cold.

  Amplitude and frequency are both contaminated by the unpowered CM4's input clamps
  conducting — with the CM4 powered, frequency is unchanged at 11.6 MHz and amplitude
  rises to 2.36 Vpp / ~896 mV rms as the clamps stop conducting. `[meas]` (Still below
  the full 3.3 V CMOS swing because the scope's 100 MHz bandwidth rounds the edges;
  crest factor 2.63 sits between square and sine, the signature of that filtering.)

  **CM4 symptom with an invalid XIN clock, confirmed `[meas]`:** power and green LEDs
  lit, static, no activity flicker. The SoC's internal PLLs never lock at 11.6 MHz so
  the boot ROM doesn't run. Worth knowing as the field diagnostic — two LEDs lit with
  no activity means no valid clock at XIN.

**This makes the Teensy lock-gating necessary, not optional.** Holding CLK4 disabled
until BIT reads locked converts this case into a clean no-clock. Drive an LED from the
BIT state at the same time and you get a positive "reference not locked" indication —
the same information the old relay's LED gave, without the relay, and better than
inferring it from a Pi that won't boot.

**Replacement, decided 2026-09-08, wired 2026-09-09 `[built]`:** BIT goes to **Teensy
GPIO pin 2**, `INPUT_PULLUP`, LOW = locked. The Teensy gates CLK4 on the Si5351's own
LOS_CLKIN / LOL_A status and uses BIT for indication only (LED + serial to the CM4),
so boot is not conditional on the AR-40A's five-minute warm-up. Nothing in the 5 V
path. Firmware: `5351.ino`.

- **Teensy 4.0 pins are NOT 5 V tolerant** — 3.3 V absolute maximum. BIT is open
  collector and only sinks, so it is safe *provided nothing else is on that line*.
  The old interlock had a relay coil between +V and BIT; if any of that wiring
  remains, an open BIT is pulled up through the coil to the relay supply and will
  destroy the input. **Verify the coil is out of circuit before powering.**
- 1 kΩ in series at the pin for ESD, 100 nF pin-to-ground to keep RF off it.
- Pin 2 chosen because it has no alternate peripheral function on a Teensy 4.0.
  Pin 10 was considered first but is SPI CS.

**Panel LEDs, wired 2026-09-09 `[built]`:** yellow on pin 3 = REF (solid when CLKIN
present and PLLA locked, blink while the PLL settles, off when there is no signal);
red on pin 4 = LOCK (on when the AR-40A reports locked). Onboard pin 13 is a
heartbeat. Both LEDs run at ~1.5 mA through 1 kΩ — Teensy 4.0 pins default to about
4 mA drive and should not be pushed. Measured Vf: yellow 1.82 V, red 1.794 V.

Wiring: one 2-conductor shielded multicore strand — the two conductors carry the
anodes, the shield is the common cathode return to Teensy ground. Resistors at the
Teensy end. The shield must not also touch the chassis; here it is a conductor, not
a shield, and bonding it to the enclosure would create the loop §5 avoids.

Together the two LEDs distinguish the failure modes the old relay's single LED could
not: REF off means a broken signal path (cable, DA or AR-40A); REF solid with LOCK
off means a working path running on an undisciplined free OCXO.

Note the colour convention is inverted from habit — red lit means *good* here. An
alternative is to drive LOCK inverted so red means "unlocked or in holdover", making
a dark panel plus yellow the healthy state.

**Optocoupler: not needed — earlier advice in this document was wrong.** The rule
about second inter-domain paths is about *low-impedance* ones: a shield bond is
milliohms and carries real current. This path sits behind a pull-up of tens of kΩ, so
it can carry only microamps, and the 10 MHz coax shield already bonds those two
grounds at low impedance regardless. A high-impedance signal path alongside an
existing low-impedance bond changes nothing.

The one case that would change it: if AR-40A ground and Teensy ground differed by
more than a few hundred mV, the saturated BIT transistor would pull the pin below the
Teensy's ground and forward-bias its ESD diode. The shield ties them, so expect
millivolts — confirmed by the AC-volts check in §7. `[?]`

