## 7. Open items

Ordered by what blocks what.

### Closed

- ~~**Measure AR-40A open-circuit output and source impedance.**~~ 2026-09-07:
  4.44 Vpp open, 46.2 Ω source, +11.25 dBm available.
- ~~**Fit the pad.**~~ 2026-09-07: DIY 7.01 dB pi, verified to 1% of prediction.
  Rebuilt 2026-09-09 with the same 150/51/150 values; confirmed indirectly by CLKIN
  measuring 2.1 Vpp clamped from an expected 2.49 Vpp, which is only reachable if the
  pad is delivering 1.245 Vpp into the DA.
- ~~**Resolve the AR-40A BIT polarity contradiction.**~~ Settled by the existing lock
  interlock — see §3.
- ~~**Check what the Si5351 breakout does at its SMA input.**~~ 2026-09-09: nothing.
  CLKIN sits at 0 V DC and clamps on the negative half (§8), so there is no bias
  network on the breakout.
- ~~**Verify the chain end to end.**~~ 2026-09-09: AR-40A → pad → DA R in → DA R out →
  CLKIN unterminated. PLLA locks, CLK4 reads 54.000 MHz, CM4 boots.
- ~~**Verify supply rails.**~~ 2026-09-09: all four within 23 mV, CM4 at 4.917 V
  booted (§5).

- ~~**Flash and verify the gating firmware.**~~ 2026-09-09: running. CLK4 gated on
  LOS_CLKIN / LOL_A; verified against a real AR-40A power cycle — reference lost,
  sticky 0xF0 confirming the drop was genuine, CLK4 disabled, then re-enabled ~1 s
  after the signal returned. BIT proven in both directions (LOCKED → UNLOCKED →
  LOCKED). Warm-up behaviour confirmed: with CLKIN present but BIT unlocked, CLK4
  stays enabled, so the CM4 is not held off during rubidium pull-in. Panel LEDs
  wired and working.

  Two bugs found and fixed along the way: a failed I2C read returned 0xFF and was
  indistinguishable from every fault bit set, so single bus glitches killed CLK4 for
  a second at a time — now a third state that holds the output; and a single-poll
  blip could disable, now debounced at two consecutive bad reads.

### Open — digital side, doable on the bench

1. **I2C bus integrity.** A single poll returned reg0=0xC1 with SYS_INIT apparently
   set while every other flag and the sticky byte were unchanged — a bit error in
   transit, not a real event. It did not trip the output only because of the
   two-read debounce. Note `i2cErr` stays at 0 for these, since the transaction
   succeeded and only the data was wrong, so the true error rate is higher than the
   counter shows. Check whether the breakout fits I2C pull-ups; the Teensy 4.0's
   internal ones are weak and 400 kHz over wire with only those is marginal. Try
   `Wire.setClock(100000)` — polling is 4 Hz, the rate is irrelevant. A software
   cross-check is also available: a fault flag set while its sticky counterpart
   reads clear is self-contradictory and can be rejected. `[?]`
2. **Cut the Teensy's VUSB pad.** It currently runs from USB alone with the PSUs
   off, so the pad is intact — connecting USB with the 5 V PSU on would tie the
   laptop's rail to the whole digital island. Until then, one supply at a time.
3. **Read back register 20** and confirm it contains 0x4F, to take the firmware out
   of any future argument about CLK4 drive strength. `[?]`
4. **Confirm the F9T breakout's I/O level** is 3.3 V and not shifted to 5 V, before
   anything touches CM4 GPIO.
5. **Decide the F9T data link:** fix USB grounding (cable GND to a header GND pin,
   short; shield terminated at the CM4 end only) or move to UART.
6. **Bring EXTINT to a bulkhead connector** while the back panel is open; TIME1 to
   the CM4 stays internal, direct, on miniature coax with 33–47 Ω series at the F9T
   end.

### Open — needs the rack move, or the AR-40A powered down

7. **Verify a DA output is a clean sine into a real 75 Ω load.** Measured clean
   unloaded (§3), which rules out voltage clipping and slew limiting. What is still
   untested is current drive into 75 Ω. *This gates the board design* — if the DA
   sags, the board's input level changes and the Extron's role has to be
   reconsidered. Mitigate by laying out footprints for either 75 Ω or 50 Ω
   termination, jumper-selected, so the board can be ordered without waiting.
8. **Confirm the Orion's actual AC termination.** Same measurement as item 7: T at
   the Orion's input, read Vpp with the Orion connected vs disconnected.
   ~1.26 / ~2.5 Vpp means it terminates at 75 Ω.
9. **AC volts between grounds** — F9T↔CM4, CM4↔Extron chassis. Take the F9T↔CM4
   reading with the 10 MHz coax disconnected as well as connected; the difference is
   the actual size of the earth-path effect. Take these with the laptop on battery
   or unplugged — a mains-connected laptop adds its own earth path through USB.
10. **Is the AR-40A chassis at mains PE?** Unplugged continuity check, enclosure to
    inlet earth pin. Determines whether the 10 MHz chain has one earth injection or
    two (§5).
11. **Resolve the CLK4 amplitude discrepancy** (§8): 1.22 Vpp and 3.4 Vpp measured
    at 54 MHz with the CM4 attached both times. Most likely the scope's 20 MHz
    bandwidth limit was enabled for the earlier reading. Worth settling, since it
    determines what the CM4's XIN is actually being fed. `[?]`
12. **Antenna:** confirm where the coax shield is bonded; if roof-mounted, a
    gas-discharge arrestor at the entry point. A GNSS DC block / galvanic isolator
    removes a whole class of ground problem, provided it passes the active antenna's
    bias.

### Shopping list

- BNC tees — none on the bench.
- 75 Ω BNC feedthrough terminator — the SDS1104X-E is 1 MΩ fixed, no switchable 50 Ω.
- SMA attenuator kit (1/2/3/6/10 dB), optional but useful for bench work.

